body:
I was very excited to download and use this app. I upgraded to the premium version because I knew I’d want to use all of the available features. I purchased this in early 2025. Since then I have been billed for the premium service, but I have yet to be able to confirm my email address or get the app to actually work past initial assessment questions.  

I would really like to use this app and it’s been almost 10 months now that I’ve been waiting for a response from customer service. Canceling is very difficult so ensure you really want this product and perhaps proactively reach out to customer service before setting up your account.

Will update my rating if customer service gets it together! Perhaps they need to use their own app for productivity! (;

This app is only useful because of notifications, but the notifications are word salad. Wanna decipher an over-edited guilt trip? Use this app. Seriously, here&#39;s an actual notification message meant to encourage app use - 

&#34;Since we parted, if you solved everything you wanted to, read no further, but if you are not yet drinking water, walking, sleeping, ie the basics, then give us anoth...&#34;

Seriously, Fabulous, I&#39;m trying to start my day by drinking water -- not with unscrambling some desperate pseudo-poetic gibberish.

I think Fabulous is, well...fabulous!  I appreciate the connections it has with the psychology of life-giving habit formation and reinforcement.  My only suggestion is that the poetic guidance you offer is both WRITTEN--and READ--as fluidly and rhythmically as &#34;The Night Before Christmas.&#34;

Thank you,

Morgan

I&#39;m normally not one to give one star reviews. But this bothered me. The app&#39;s ad for the paid version includes this highlighted quote:

&#34;I&#39;m on a limited income. Due to my tight budget, yesterday I was so concerned I might live beyond my means that I thought about canceling the trial subscriptions-but then I realized that this subscription could potentially be the beginning of my path back to health.&#34;

The app&#39;s producers are trying to convince people with limited income to go ahead and buy premium. Given that it&#39;s highly probable that people who use habit building apps won&#39;t continue with them for long, this seems ethically questionable.
